The order of processing arguments is NOT defined in C, C++ or most
other languages.  Many architectures expect the arguments to be placed
on the stack in reverse order *but not all* architectures.  Many
function libraries expect the arguments to be placed on the stack in
reverse order *but not all* function libraries.  Also, the order of
execution of side effects is NOT mandated to be in the order of the
parameters.  The idea of pre-increment and the idea of parameter-pass
don't automatically play together like what you saw.

